\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces Diagnostic plots for the final linear regression model. The residuals-versus-fitted and scale-location plots suggest roughly constant variance overall, although there is small non-constant spread at the extremes. The Q-Q plot shows a consistent trend, with little deviation even at the extremes, meaning the assumption of normality is unlikely violated.}}{8}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces Residuals plotted against covariates in the final linear regression model. The residuals-versus-income plot shows some non-constant variance at the extreme lower and upper ends. The residuals-versus-prime-interest-rate plot shows a more even spread, supporting the constant variance assumption for this predictor. The residuals-housing-type boxplots show a similar spread across categories.}}{8}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {6}{\ignorespaces Sequential residual plot for the final linear regression model. The wave-like structure suggests that the errors are not fully independent over time, indicating that the current model may be inadequate.}}{9}{figure.6}\protected@file@percent }
